{"reference":"Job 14","book":"Job","chapter":14,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Job 14 in the Dialogue","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Job 14 belongs to Job’s sustained debate with Eliphaz, Bildad, and Zophar. After they first sat with him in silence for seven days, the friends increasingly explain Job’s suffering as evidence of wrongdoing, while Job rejects that diagnosis. This wider exchange helps distinguish Job’s anguished speech here from the friends’ attempts to account for it.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_014_c6faa852f378891e5c2acd0e"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"Job’s endurance across the story can describe persistence through suffering and its eventual outcome. It should not be read as a demand for silent endurance of abuse or as a barrier to seeking escape and protection. That distinction matters when this chapter gives full voice to Job’s pain, protest, and uncertainty.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_014_e9701d04ac5a06d3d148e770"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}